1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:24,455
Six months after the wedding, my wife sent me out for butter. "Just
butter." "Nigga, why you putting security around the noun?" "Because I
sent you for lemons and you bought part of a restaurant." "Excellent
lemons." She pointed her wooden spoon. "Butter." "Salted?" "Yes."
"See? Nigga, communication saves marriages." "Coming back before I
finish cooking would also help." She kissed me.

2
00:00:24,705 --> 00:00:48,187
I let the kiss finish before I tried to defend the restaurant. That
was six months of progress right there. The house smelled of coffee.
The morning was warm. Outside, the white horse watched the gardener
arrange flowers with the bored authority of a nigga whose breakfast
had never depended on an alarm clock. My shoulder had healed. My arm
had healed.

3
00:00:48,437 --> 00:01:09,983
Vega had cleared me for ordinary activity and refused to define
ordinary where I could get a witness. The Last Word waited in the
drive, blue paint shining, silver lettering clean, Shawna's name
inside the passenger door. I got behind the wheel and started the
engine. Nigga, there are men who leave the house.

4
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:34,974
Then there's the conversation my engine has with the neighborhood
about it. The gardener looked up. "Morning, Captain." "Morning, my
nigga. You need anything?" "Roses arrive this afternoon." "Get extra."
"Your wife already did." I grinned. Nigga had no idea how much I liked
living somewhere my best thought had already occurred to somebody
else. One word on the grocery list.

5
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:58,115
Salted underneath it in my wife's handwriting. She knew which portions
of my genius required documentation. Tank called before the first
light. "Brother." "Husband." The nigga still laughed at that. Every
time. Big dangerous motherfucker carrying a wedding ring like somebody
had finally given him proof he could keep. "Rosa says come tonight.

6
00:01:58,365 --> 00:02:10,547
She's trying a new song." "Nigga, last song had you crying into
pasta." "Sauce was hot." "Emotionally?" "You bringing Shawna or
interrogating dinner?" "We'll be there." "Good.

7
00:02:10,797 --> 00:02:30,187
Also, I got a car." "Nigga, why hide that behind the invitation?"
"Rosa says lead with relationships." From somewhere beside him: "I
said stop introducing the car before me!" "Baby, I said your name
first!" "You said my name so you could get to the car!" I laughed
through the green light.

8
00:02:30,436 --> 00:02:48,537
"My nigga, let me speak to my sister." "Traitor," he said, and handed
her the phone. "Bring Shawna," Rosa told me. "And tell your brother he
can love two things without announcing their engine sizes." "I'm
buying butter. I got my own supervision." She laughed.

9
00:02:48,787 --> 00:03:12,371
I promised we'd come and ended the call before Tank could tell me how
fast his new relationship went from zero to sixty. At the bakery, two
men were robbing the register. Nigga, two grown motherfuckers in black
masks stood between me and salted butter at nine-fifteen in the
morning. One held a gun. The other held a pastry bag. I stopped in the
doorway.

10
00:03:12,621 --> 00:03:35,480
The woman behind the counter looked at me. Then at them. Then she
sighed with such relief that the nigga holding the gun turned around
to see what she had discovered. "Everybody stay—" He stopped. I took
off my sunglasses. His friend looked up from the pastry bag. "Nigga."
"Yeah," I said. "Morning." The gunman's eyes widened above the mask.

11
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:58,869
"Aiden?" "Captain, while you doing that." The other man lowered the
bag. "Nigga, I told you we should've gone across town." "You told me
the croissants were good!" "They are good! That doesn't make this
nigga less here!" I looked at the baker. "You all right?" She nodded.
"They haven't hurt anybody." "Good. Let's keep that little achievement
intact." I held out my hand.

12
00:03:59,119 --> 00:04:22,153
The gunman stared at it. "What?" "Nigga, give me the gun." His friend
nodded urgently. "Give the nigga the gun." "Shut up." "You shut up.
That's the nigga from the bridge." I waited. The gunman looked at the
photograph of me beside the register. The baker had put it there after
I paid for a year's worth of pastries for the officers working night
shifts.

13
00:04:22,403 --> 00:04:45,107
In the photograph I held a croissant like a man presenting an
important discovery to science. The nigga looked from the photograph
back to me. "You really live here." "Nigga, what you think I do? Hover
over the city between incidents?" His friend began laughing nervously.
The gunman handed me the weapon. Then a third man burst out of the
back room.

14
00:04:45,357 --> 00:05:06,402
Of course there was a third nigga. Bad planning loves company. He saw
me, saw his friends surrendering, and ran straight through the side
door. I looked at the two men in front of me. "Stay." They stayed. The
baker reached beneath the counter and brought out the butter. "I'll
hold this." "Appreciate you." I ran outside.

15
00:05:06,652 --> 00:05:29,010
The third man jumped into a stolen delivery car and pulled away hard
enough to scatter a row of empty crates. I got into the Last Word, hit
the lights, and followed. Nigga, my wife had given me one item. One.
The delivery car turned onto the boulevard. Ahead, a slow procession
of trucks moved toward the civic plaza.

16
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:52,328
Workers were bringing in equipment for a public celebration I had
forgotten to put on my calendar because apparently the city kept
finding new reasons to honor me without checking whether I had
breakfast plans. On the largest truck stood a covered statue. I
recognized the shape beneath the sheet. "Nigga," I muttered. The thief
swerved between the trucks.

17
00:05:52,578 --> 00:06:18,029
I followed, close enough to see him looking at me in the mirror with
growing spiritual concern. He took the next turn too fast. His car
clipped the statue truck's rear corner. The straps snapped. The sheet
flew off. A twenty-foot bronze Aiden rolled backward onto its
transport platform, one enormous arm pointing at the sky. Nigga, I
looked excellent. The truck driver braked.

18
00:06:18,279 --> 00:06:40,027
The statue slid toward the open end. The thief's car stopped beneath
its outstretched boot. He looked up. Then at me pulling alongside.
Then up again. "You gotta be fucking kidding me," he said. I lowered
my window. "Nigga, even my statue caught you." The statue's boot
settled against his hood with a deep metallic groan.

19
00:06:40,277 --> 00:07:03,126
The front of the delivery car folded enough to trap it in place. The
thief sat frozen behind the windshield while the largest version of my
shoe in existence held him politely against the road. Nobody was hurt.
Several people were already filming. The nigga opened his door very
slowly and raised both hands. "I surrender." "To which one of me?" He
stared.

20
00:07:03,376 --> 00:07:23,502
I pointed up at the statue. "Nigga, jurisdiction complicated today."
The crowd began laughing. Knox arrived in her own car five minutes
later. She looked at the bronze boot on the stolen vehicle. Then at
me. Then at the sculptor standing nearby with both hands pressed to
his head.

21
00:07:23,752 --> 00:07:48,600
"Captain," she said, "I am glad to see you contributing even while
stationary." "Efficient department." The thief looked between us.
"Y'all always like this?" Knox put a hand on his shoulder.
"Unfortunately for your morning, yes." She took him away. The sculptor
approached me, trembling. "The heel may be damaged." I looked up at my
bronze face. "Nigga, the cheekbones survived.

22
00:07:48,850 --> 00:08:11,106
We're ahead." He laughed, then almost cried, then asked whether I
would stand beside the statue for a photograph before the recovery
crew moved it. I did. The crowd cheered. Somebody shouted,
"Niggalicious!" Three other niggas shouted it back. I pointed toward
them. "Educated citizens." My phone rang. Shawna. I answered.

23
00:08:11,356 --> 00:08:35,824
"Baby." "Butter." "I got it." "I'm watching a twenty-foot version of
you arrest a man on my phone." "Then you understand the delay."
"Aiden." "Salted. I remembered." She tried not to laugh. I heard her
fail. Nigga, that sound could still make a whole city move to the
background. I went back to the bakery. Knox's officers had taken the
first two men away.

24
00:08:36,074 --> 00:08:59,715
The baker stood behind the counter with the butter wrapped in paper
and a bag of pastries beside it. "For your wife," she said. "She sent
me for one thing." "Tell her I insisted." "Nigga, excellent witness."
The woman laughed. I paid, left more than the bill required, and drove
home without purchasing the bakery, the block, or any additional part
of municipal government.

25
00:08:59,965 --> 00:09:21,728
Growth, if a nigga wanted to call it that. At the house, Shawna stood
in the kitchen wearing one of my shirts and an expression she had
clearly been practicing since the video appeared. I held up the
butter. She looked at it. Then at the pastries. Then at me. "You made
it." "Nigga, against all odds." She came around the counter and kissed
me.

26
00:09:21,978 --> 00:09:43,537
Not because the city had cheered. Not because a statue of me now
required a new heel. Because I had come through the door and she
wanted me close. I put the groceries down and held her until the pan
on the stove reminded us breakfast still existed. We ate on the
terrace. The garden moved in the breeze.

27
00:09:43,787 --> 00:10:08,428
The white horse grazed beyond the fence. Somewhere inside, my phone
kept filling with photographs of bronze greatness and one very
frightened thief. I left it there. Shawna buttered her toast and
watched me over her coffee. "What?" I asked. "You look happy." I
considered making a joke. Then I let the simple thing stay simple for
one breath. "I am." She smiled.

28
00:10:08,678 --> 00:10:31,055
After breakfast, we opened the messages from our friends. Rosa sent a
recording of the new song. Tank could be heard humming badly in the
background until she laughed and made him start again. Cassius and
Inez sent a photograph from their racing garage, both fathers standing
behind them arguing over a car nobody had asked them to fix.

29
00:10:31,305 --> 00:10:55,498
Their first team driver had won a race that morning. Cassius wrote
that victory tasted better when the woman beside him stole his
champagne afterward. Liora sent the First Dance above the sea, full of
paying passengers and musicians on their way to Sienna's opening
festival. The heart remained on the side. Tank had agreed to share the
naming rights with love in general.

30
00:10:55,748 --> 00:11:21,594
June sent the rebuilt Crownless Hotel's front doors. Bernard stood
between the two courtyard palms with Magnolia's hand in his. Her cane
leaned against his leg. The new sign gleamed behind them. Vesper sent
a photograph from the Blue House. Bastien sat beside Madame Caron,
looking annoyed by the camera and pleased by everything else. Noor and
Salma had come for dinner with their mother.

31
00:11:21,844 --> 00:11:44,199
Nobody in the picture appeared to be awaiting permission to leave.
Victor sent a picture of my academy portrait. The faculty had made it
larger. Nigga, I approved. Marcellus sent no photograph. He called. He
had opened the workshop early. Sunlight was good at that hour, he
said. Blue Mercy shone on his hand while he worked.

32
00:11:44,449 --> 00:12:09,580
Celeste's letter remained in the drawer where he could reach it. He
had begun teaching two grown apprentices who asked good questions and
held their tools badly, a combination he described with obvious
delight. Isolde taught dancing in the room above the workshop and came
downstairs to steal his coffee, correct his posture, and kiss him
before the apprentices could pretend they had not noticed.

33
00:12:09,830 --> 00:12:30,631
"Come by next week," he told us. "I have something for the house."
"Nigga, we got enough jewels." He laughed. "A clock. You two are
always late." Shawna took the phone to defend herself. I listened to
them argue and leaned back in my chair. Gideon and Orson were in
prison. Their cases had ended months earlier.

34
00:12:30,881 --> 00:12:54,012
Zero lay in a grave in the desert, named plainly, with no throne above
him and no unfinished business beneath the stone. The people who had
followed him had made their choices, served their sentences, changed
their work, or gone home. None of them was waiting in a dark room to
become the last page of my life. The last page was here.

35
00:12:54,262 --> 00:13:16,681
My wife on the terrace. My brother calling about dinner. My city loud
beyond the walls. Butter on the table. Aight, my nigga. Now look at
us. That nigga Tank got a ring on his hand and a woman who makes him
dance. That nigga Victor still thinks he can beat me at something, and
I love the nigga enough to let him keep trying.

36
00:13:16,931 --> 00:13:37,652
Cassius got Inez beside him, and if that nigga acts surprised one more
time when happiness climbs into the passenger seat, I'm charging him
for the lesson. Marcellus got his stone, his letter, his good light in
the morning. Nigga, the old man got mornings. Look at my people. My
niggas got keys.

37
00:13:37,902 --> 00:14:00,714
My niggas got places to put their shoes down without wondering who
might make them run. Rosa got a stage and a husband who learns the
words. Mercedes and Porsche got a room with their names over the door,
and niggas better behave inside it. June got engines worth arguing
with. Liora got a whole fucking sky that doesn't belong to an emperor.

38
00:14:00,964 --> 00:14:24,143
Vesper got friends who can tell her when she's being difficult and
stay for dinner afterward. And me? Nigga, look at me. I'm still the
man who likes the loud car. Still the nigga who sees a crown and wants
to know how it looks. Still the captain who should occasionally be
supervised by somebody with better judgment and smaller ambitions for
a grocery run.

39
00:14:24,393 --> 00:14:44,809
I got guns in the safe, money in places a nigga could get lost trying
to count, and a city that put my face in bronze before asking whether
bronze could handle the responsibility. I did not become quiet. Nigga,
you know me better than that. I became somebody with a reason to hear
the door open.

40
00:14:45,059 --> 00:15:07,557
Because that's my wife coming out onto the terrace now. That's Shawna
saying my name. Nigga, the way she says it still does something money
never learned. She doesn't say king. She doesn't say captain. She says
Aiden like the whole loud motherfucker fits inside a word she can
carry without getting tired. I get up. She puts her hand in mine.

41
00:15:07,807 --> 00:15:29,856
"What you smiling at?" she asks. "Nigga," I tell her, "I'm thinking
about how good your taste is." She laughs. The same woman. The same
laugh. New morning. That's my heart, my nigga. Not somewhere precious
where nobody can touch it. Right here, warm hand in my hand, telling
me I bought too many pastries while eating the second one.

42
00:15:30,106 --> 00:15:51,883
Ten toes down for the niggas who came when I called. Ten toes down for
the women who called me on my shit and loved me with their eyes open.
Ten toes down in the kitchen, in the driveway, beside my brother,
beside my wife. Nigga, standing is easy when you finally know where
you want to stay. I came back. She was here.

43
00:15:52,133 --> 00:15:58,165
And the rest of the morning belonged to us. Aiden. Ten toes down.
Forever, my nigga.
